From 76004b7419d54b7d66cd15172e2ed2e1ed14b5f9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <claude@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 04:28:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat: Add calibrated escalation via Selective Guaranteed Risk
 (SGR)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Replaces the hand-picked ESCALATION_THRESHOLD constant with an optional
statistically-calibrated threshold, computed from labeled calibration
data using the Selective Guaranteed Risk procedure (Geifman & El-Yaniv,
NeurIPS 2017).

- ai_core/calibration/selective_risk.py: pure-stdlib SGR algorithm
  (Hoeffding-bounded risk), zero new dependencies
- ai_core/calibration/{models,store,provider}.py: artifact persistence
  and CalibratedThresholdProvider with safe fallback to the static
  config threshold when no valid calibration exists
- scripts/collect_calibration_data.py: runs real IntentAgent against
  labeled examples to produce calibration data (needs live LLM creds)
- scripts/compute_calibrated_threshold.py: pure statistics, no network,
  turns calibration data into a threshold artifact
- DecisionEngine.should_escalate() now goes through
  CalibratedThresholdProvider instead of a hardcoded threshold
- 42 real labeled seed examples across all 7 intent types, plus
  clearly-labeled synthetic demo data proving the pipeline end-to-end
- 9 new tests (5 algorithm-level, 4 integration-level), all passing;
  all pre-existing decision-engine tests (8) still pass unchanged —
  backward compatible by construction: no calibrated_thresholds.json
  ships by default, so behavior is identical until someone deliberately
  runs the calibration pipeline
- CALIBRATION.md documents the method, its honest limitations at small
  sample sizes, and the empirical study needed to turn this into a real
  novelty claim rather than just a mechanism
